This walk-through builds the smallest useful Mortar site: one brick, a `pages` collection, a route that renders a page, and the admin.

## 1. Define a brick

A brick is a component plus the schema of its data. The schema is built with `fields.*` helpers, and the component receives the data, already validated and with defaults filled in, as `props.data`.

```tsx
// src/bricks/hero.tsx
import { defineBrick, fields } from '@mortar-kit/core';

export const hero = defineBrick({
  name: 'hero',
  label: 'Hero',
  schema: {
    heading: fields.text({ label: 'Heading', required: true, default: 'Hello' }),
    intro: fields.textarea({ label: 'Intro' }),
  },
  component: (props) => (
    <section>
      <h1>{props.data.heading}</h1>
      <p>{props.data.intro}</p>
    </section>
  ),
});

export const bricks = [hero];
```

## 2. Declare a collection

Pages aren't built in: a site declares its own `pages` collection. With `body: true`, each page also has an ordered list of bricks.

```ts
// src/collections.ts
import { defineCollection, fields } from '@mortar-kit/core';

export const pages = defineCollection({
  name: 'pages',
  label: 'Pages',
  fields: {
    title: fields.text({ label: 'Title', required: true }),
  },
  body: true,
  url: '/:slug',
  home: 'home',
  reservedSlugs: ['admin'],
});

export const collections = [pages];
```

## 3. Store content

`createContent` applies Mortar's rules over a store. On the server, use the JSON-file store, and let `createContentServer` turn it into functions the browser can call. Reads are open to everyone; writes need a signed-in editor once you add [sign-in](/docs/sign-in) (`auth: false` leaves them open while you try things out locally):

```ts
// src/server/mortar.ts
import { createContent } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { createContentServer, createJsonFileStore } from '@mortar-kit/core/server';

import { bricks } from '../bricks/hero.tsx';
import { collections } from '../collections.ts';

export const content = createContent({ store: createJsonFileStore({ dir: 'content' }), collections, bricks });
export const server = createContentServer({ content, auth: false });

// src/server/content.ts
'use server';
import { server } from './mortar.ts';

export const list = server.list;
export const get = server.get;
export const save = server.save;
export const remove = server.remove;
export const move = server.move;
```

The other functions it makes (`listAssets`, `uploadAsset` and the rest) are for [assets](/docs/assets); see [Storage](/docs/storage#calling-it-from-the-browser) for the full list. A page is now a file such as `content/pages/home.json`.

Then build the browser side with `contentClient`: cached reads for your pages, fresh reads and writes for the admin:

```ts
// src/content.ts
import { contentClient } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { query } from '@solidjs/router';
import { GET } from '@solidjs/web/server-functions';

import * as server from './server/content.ts';

export const { source, freshSource, content: adminContent } = contentClient(server, { read: GET, cache: query, onWrite: () => query.clear() });
```

## 4. Render a page

Provide the reads to your app with `ContentContext`:

```tsx
// src/App.tsx
import { ContentContext } from '@mortar-kit/core';

import { source } from './content.ts';

export default function App() {
  return <ContentContext value={source}>{/* your router */}</ContentContext>;
}
```

Then read a document with `useContent()` and render its body with `<PageView>`:

```tsx
// src/routes/page.tsx
import { PageView, useContent } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { createMemo, Show } from 'solid-js';

import { bricks } from '../bricks/hero.tsx';
import { pages } from '../collections.ts';

export function PageRoute(props: { slug: string }) {
  const content = useContent();
  const page = createMemo(() => content.get(pages, { slug: props.slug }));
  return (
    <Show when={page()}>
      {(p) => <PageView bricks={bricks} entries={p().entries ?? []} />}
    </Show>
  );
}
```

## 5. Mount the admin

The admin is a route in your app. It needs reads and writes, and the URL of a preview route that renders `<PreviewPage>`:

```tsx
// src/routes/admin.tsx
import { Admin } from '@mortar-kit/admin';

export default function AdminRoute() {
  return <Admin bricks={bricks} collections={collections} content={adminContent} previewUrl="/admin/preview" />;
}

// src/routes/admin-preview.tsx
import { PreviewPage } from '@mortar-kit/admin/preview';

export default function AdminPreview() {
  return <PreviewPage bricks={bricks} content={freshSource} />;
}
```

`adminContent` and `freshSource` come from `contentClient`: the admin reads fresh, so it always shows what is saved, while your pages keep their cached reads. Load both routes lazily, so admin code stays out of your public pages' bundle.
